  "response_type": "answer",
  "short_answer": "Dietary iodine deficiency prevents the thyroid gland from synthesizing sufficient thyroid hormones (T3 and T4). The resulting low levels of circulating thyroid hormones stimulate the pituitary gland to continuously secrete high levels of Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH). While TSH attempts to stimulate the thyroid to produce hormones, the lack of iodine means hormone production remains blocked, but the chronic TSH elevation drives excessive cellular growth and proliferation of thyroid follicular cells (hypertrophy and hyperplasia), leading to an enlarged thyroid gland known as a goiter."
}

